{
  "_comment": [
    "Single source of truth for how the XCUITest suites are tiered and sharded.",
    "",
    "TIERS",
    "  component  focused: one test drives one feature and asserts on it.",
    "  smoke      broad: one test walks a path that joins several features",
    "             (interaction capture + session context + export + schema).",
    "  soak       leak probes, run through tool/run_leak_harness.sh (harness: true).\n             The tests themselves only assert the screen appeared \u2014 the actual\n             leak check is a pixel scan of frames captured by the harness's mock\n             upload server, so running them WITHOUT the harness proves nothing.\n             A harness shard must hold exactly one test; the plan job enforces it.\n\n  quarantine  registered so the coverage guard is satisfied, but in no tier that\n              ci.yml runs \u2014 so it does not execute at all. For a test that is\n              working correctly and reporting a defect nobody has scheduled:\n              running it would only produce a red check people learn to ignore.\n              Move it back to its real tier when the defect is fixed.\n             Formerly described as cheap enough to gate a\n             PR once split one-test-per-shard, so they ride under the slowest\n             smoke shard rather than extending it.",
    "",
    "Shards run as parallel jobs against one shared build, so wall-clock is the",
    "SLOWEST shard, not the sum. `seconds` is the measured runtime of the tests",
    "in that shard (GitHub macos-15, run 33612331880) and exists so the packing",
    "can be rebalanced from data rather than guesswork. Keep shards under ~200s.",
    "",
    "Adding a UI test? Add it to a shard here or it will not run in CI.",
    "The tier-coverage check in ci-ui-tests.yml fails the build if a test method",
    "exists in the source but is missing from this file."
  ],
